---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Program = `let classNames = [];
let averages = [];

let howMany = Number(prompt("How many classes?"));

for (let i = 0; i < howMany; i++)
{
  let name = prompt("Class name:");
  let score = Number(prompt("Current average:"));
  classNames.push(name);
  averages.push(score);
}

function classesBelow(cutoff)
{
  let count = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < classNames.length; i++)
  {
    if (averages[i] < cutoff)
    {
      console.log("  " + classNames[i] + " - " + averages[i]);
      count++;
    }
  }
  return count;
}

console.log("");
console.log("Below 70:");
let failing = classesBelow(70);
console.log("  " + failing + " class(es)");

console.log("");
console.log("Below 80:");
let shaky = classesBelow(80);
console.log("  " + shaky + " class(es)");`;

const Run = `How many classes? 4 [Enter]
Class name: Algebra 2 [Enter]
Current average: 84 [Enter]
Class name: Chemistry [Enter]
Current average: 68 [Enter]
Class name: US History [Enter]
Current average: 77 [Enter]
Class name: Spanish 3 [Enter]
Current average: 91 [Enter]

Below 70:
  Chemistry - 68
  1 class(es)

Below 80:
  Chemistry - 68
  US History - 77
  2 class(es)`;

const FilledSpec = `PROGRAM: Grade Check

WHAT IT DOES, in two sentences:
  You type in each class and your current average.
  It tells you which of them are below a cutoff.

WHO WOULD USE IT, and what they need from it:
  Me, the night before progress reports, to work out
  which class to worry about first.

INPUT      how many classes, then a name and an average
           for each one
LIST       classNames and averages, one entry per class,
           and with no list there is nothing to search
PROCEDURE  classesBelow
PARAMETER  cutoff. Pass 70 and you get one class back.
           Pass 80 and you get two
INSIDE IT  the loop ....... over every class in the list
           the decision ... is this average below cutoff
OUTPUT     the classes under the cutoff, and how many`;

const OverScoped = `PROGRAM: StudySphere

WHAT IT DOES, in two sentences:
  A study planner with accounts, a calendar, automatic
  grade import, reminders, a dashboard and a phone app.

WHO WOULD USE IT, and what they need from it:
  Every student in the school.

INPUT      log in, courses, assignments, due dates ...
LIST       users, courses, assignments, reminders ...
PROCEDURE  about forty of them
PARAMETER  ...
INSIDE IT  ...
OUTPUT     ...

SESSIONS YOU HAVE ...... 8
SESSIONS THIS NEEDS .... considerably more than 8

SCORE IF IT IS FINISHED ......... same 6 as Grade Check
SCORE IF IT IS NOT FINISHED ..... whatever the half-built
                                  version can prove`;

const NearMiss = `function isBelow(score, cutoff)
{
  if (score < cutoff)
  {
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

console.log("");
console.log("Below 70:");
let failing = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < classNames.length; i++)
{
  if (isBelow(averages[i], 70))
  {
    console.log("  " + classNames[i] + " - " + averages[i]);
    failing++;
  }
}
console.log("  " + failing + " class(es)");`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Sample Project: How Small Is Big Enough</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Reference &mdash; Read Before You Scope</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Why This Page Exists</h2>

          <p>
            Read the six requirements and it is easy to picture an app. Accounts,
            a database, a dashboard, something with a logo.{" "}
            <b>The requirements do not ask for that.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            Below is a complete program that meets <b>all six</b>. It is{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-cyan">36 lines</b>, it took one sitting to write, and
            somebody could use it in about ninety seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is not a template and you cannot hand it in.</b> Work that comes
            from a teacher's sample, a curriculum provider's sample, or a practice
            task may not be submitted as your own. It is here so you have something
            to <i>measure against</i> &mdash; a floor, not a ceiling, and definitely
            not a starting file.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="program">
          <h2>The Whole Program</h2>

          <Code
            code={Program}
            frame="code"
            lang="js"
            title="Grade Check"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>That is all of it.</b> Nothing was left out to make it fit on the
            page.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="run">
          <h2>What It Does</h2>

          <Code
            code={Run}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="text"
            title="Grade Check"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Look at the two answers.</b> The same procedure was called twice with
            a different number and gave back a different list. That is not a nice
            touch &mdash; it is the requirement, made visible.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="map">
          <h2>Where Each Requirement Lives</h2>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-dark table-striped align-middle">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Requirement</th>
                  <th>Line</th>
                  <th>What satisfies it</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>1 Input</b></td>
                  <td><code>4, 8, 9</code></td>
                  <td>
                    Three questions, and every answer ends up in the lists that
                    everything else reads
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>2 List</b></td>
                  <td><code>1&ndash;2, 10&ndash;11</code></td>
                  <td>
                    <code>classNames</code> and <code>averages</code>. Delete them
                    and there is nothing left to search
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>3 Procedure</b></td>
                  <td><code>14</code></td>
                  <td>
                    <code>classesBelow(cutoff)</code>, written by the student, with
                    a parameter that changes the result
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>4 Algorithm</b></td>
                  <td><code>16&ndash;25</code></td>
                  <td>
                    Sequencing, <b>selection</b> at line 19, and <b>iteration</b> at
                    line 17 &mdash; all three <i>inside the procedure's body</i>
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>5 Calls</b></td>
                  <td><code>30, 35</code></td>
                  <td>Called twice, with different arguments</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>6 Output</b></td>
                  <td><code>21, 31, 36</code></td>
                  <td>
                    Depends on what was typed in and on which cutoff was passed
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            <b>Requirement 4 is the one that fails silently</b>, and it is worth
            saying exactly what it asks: the loop and the decision have to be{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-cyan">inside the procedure you wrote</b>. Not
            somewhere in the program. Inside those braces.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="proposal">
          <h2>The Proposal That Produced It</h2>

          <p>
            This is the same planning sheet you fill in at the scoping gate, filled
            in for the program above. Notice how little there is to say.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={FilledSpec}
            frame="code"
            lang="text"
            title="Proposal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="overscoped">
          <h2>And the Proposal Most People Write First</h2>

          <Code
            code={OverScoped}
            frame="code"
            lang="text"
            title="Proposal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Finished, this scores exactly what Grade Check scores.</b> There is no
            bonus for ambition. The rubric asks whether a list is there, whether a
            procedure is there, whether the loop and the decision are inside it
            &mdash; and a 32-line program answers yes just as loudly as a 3,000-line
            one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Unfinished, it scores whatever the half-built version can prove</b>,
            which is usually much less. That is the entire risk, and it is
            avoidable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The honest version of the advice:</b> pick something you could
            describe to somebody in one sentence, build it until it works, and then
            &mdash; only then &mdash; add a second feature if the time is there.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="nearmiss">
          <h2>The Version That Loses a Point</h2>

          <p>
            Here is the same program with one change. It runs. It produces{" "}
            <b>character-for-character the same output</b> as the version above.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={NearMiss}
            frame="code"
            lang="js"
            title="Grade Check, changed"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>The loop moved out of the procedure.</b> <code>isBelow</code> is a
            real procedure and it does have a parameter that changes what it does
            &mdash; but its body has only a decision. No loop. So{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-cyan">requirement 4 is not met</b>, and the program
            you see on screen is identical.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is why the requirement is the one that fails silently.</b> You
            cannot catch it by running the program. You catch it by opening the
            procedure and looking for all three things between its braces.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="rules">
          <h2>What You May and May Not Do With This</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Read it, take it apart, and run your own version of the idea.</b>{" "}
              That is what it is for.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Use it as a size check.</b> If your plan is more than a few times
              this big, say so at the gate and cut it down early.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Do not submit it, or a lightly edited copy of it.</b> Work taken
              from a sample and handed in as your own is not your own.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Do not pick the same idea.</b> Grade Check is deliberately dull.
              Yours should be something you actually want.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Your program has to be yours.</b> Not because copying is hard to
            catch &mdash; because on exam day you write about your own code, from
            memory, with only your Project Reference in front of you. A program you
            did not write is a program you cannot describe.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Smaller Than You Think
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
